#### Headings

Use `#` symbols to create headings. One `#` is a top-level heading, six `######` is the smallest.

```markdown
# Installation Guide
## System Requirements
### Windows
### macOS
## Configuration
#### Network Settings
```


#### Text Styling

| Style | Syntax | Example |
|-------|--------|---------|
| Bold | `**text**` or `__text__` | **bold text** |
| Italic | `*text*` or `_text_` | *italic text* |
| Bold and italic | `***text***` | ***bold and italic*** |
| Strikethrough | `~~text~~` | ~~strikethrough text~~ |

**Note:** GFM ignores underscores within words (e.g., `wow_great_stuff` renders without becoming italic), so you can safely use underscores in variable names and code.


#### Lists

**Unordered lists** (use `-`, `*`, or `+`):

```markdown
- First item
- Second item
  - Indented sub-item
  - Another sub-item
- Third item
```

**Ordered lists** (use numbers followed by a period):

```markdown
1. Open the application
2. Select the settings menu
3. Click Save
    1. Confirm the changes
    2. Restart if prompted
4. Verify the update
```


#### Links

**Inline links:**

```markdown
[Docs Assembler documentation](https://netoftrees.com)
```

**Automatic links:** GFM automatically converts URLs into clickable links.

```markdown
https://netoftrees.com
```


#### Images

```markdown
![Alt text describing the image](./assets/screenshot.png)
```

> **Tip:** In Docs Assembler, image paths are often stored as variables rather than hard-coded strings. This lets the system track and resolve the path correctly when files are moved, copied, or published. See [Variables and Imports] for details.


#### Blockquotes

Use `>` to create quoted text.

```markdown
> Important: Save your changes before proceeding.
>
> This applies to all users.
```


#### Code

**Inline code:** Use backticks for code or commands within a sentence.

```markdown
Press `Ctrl+S` to save the file.
```

**Fenced code blocks:** Use three backticks ` ``` ` to create a code block. Unlike standard Markdown, you don't need to indent by four spaces.

````markdown
```
npm install
npm run build
```
````

**Syntax highlighting:** Add a language identifier after the opening backticks.

````markdown
```json
{
  "name": "example",
  "version": "1.0.0"
}
```
````


##### Common language identifiers

**Docs Assembler / Web:** `markdown`, `html`, `css`, `javascript`, `js`, `json`, `jsonc`, `typescript`, `ts`, `yaml`, `xml`

**Shell / Ops:** `bash`, `shell`, `powershell`, `ps1`, `dockerfile`, `ini`

**Data:** `csv`, `sql`, `regex`

**General:** `python`, `java`, `csharp`, `cpp`, `rust`, `go`, `ruby`, `php`

*Tip: If unsure, `text` is always safe and will not apply any highlighting.*


#### Tables

Create tables using pipes (`|`) and hyphens (`-`).

```markdown
| Feature | Description | Status |
|---------|-------------|--------|
| Import | Load external files | ✅ Complete |
| Export | Generate output | 🚧 In progress |
| Validate | Check for errors | ✅ Complete |
```

**Column alignment:**

| Alignment | Syntax |
|-----------|--------|
| Left | `:---` |
| Center | `:---:` |
| Right | `---:` |

```markdown
| Left | Center | Right |
|:-----|:------:|------:|
| Text | Text   | Text  |
```

##### Advanced table tips & troubleshooting

**Line breaks inside a cell:** Use `<br>` HTML:

```markdown
| Step | Action |
|------|--------|
| 1 | Open the menu<br>Click Settings |
```

**Merged cells:** GFM does not support `colspan` or `rowspan` in pure Markdown. Use raw HTML if you need merged cells.

**Table not rendering?** Common causes:
- Missing blank line before the table
- Inconsistent column counts across rows
- Missing leading or trailing pipes on some rows


#### Task Lists

Create interactive checklists using `- [ ]` for incomplete items and `- [x]` for completed items.

```markdown
- [x] Install the extension
- [ ] Configure the settings
- [ ] Test the publish workflow
- [ ] Review the output
```


#### Horizontal Rules

Use three or more hyphens, asterisks, or underscores on a new line.

```markdown
---
```


#### Escaping Special Characters

Use a backslash (`\`) to display literal characters that would otherwise be interpreted as Markdown syntax or Docs Assembler directives.

```markdown
\*This is not italic\*
\_This is not italic either\_
\# This is not a heading
```

This is essential when writing documentation *about* Docs Assembler syntax. For example, if you want to show the literal text `¬{{::il.logo_image}}` or `¬import` without triggering variable resolution or import processing, escaping ensures the characters appear exactly as typed.

##### Full list of escapable characters

| Character | Name |
|-----------|------|
| `\` | backslash |
| `` ` `` | backtick |
| `*` | asterisk |
| `_` | underscore |
| `{ }` | curly braces |
| `[ ]` | square brackets |
| `< >` | angle brackets |
| `( )` | parentheses |
| `#` | hash |
| `+` | plus |
| `-` | minus / hyphen |
| `.` | dot |
| `!` | exclamation mark |
| `|` | pipe |


#### Raw HTML

GFM allows inline HTML when you need markup that Markdown syntax cannot express — for example, complex table layouts or image attributes like `width`.

```markdown
<img src="./assets/diagram.png" width="400" alt="System diagram">
```

Use this sparingly; standard Markdown is preferred for portability.

